---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CurrencyConverter from '@/components/tools/reference/CurrencyConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="currency-converter">
  <CurrencyConverter client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the currency converter works</h2>
    <p>
      The currency converter uses the euro foreign exchange reference rates of the European Central
      Bank. Each rate says how many units of a currency one euro buys. To convert between two other
      currencies, for example dollars to pounds, the converter divides one euro rate by the other:
      <code>GBP per USD = (GBP per EUR) / (USD per EUR)</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ECB sets the rates once each working day at about 16:00 CET from a regular check of the
      market. This site downloads them every day, checks them, and adds them to the page, so the
      converter works offline and your amounts stay in the browser.
    </p>
    <h2>Exchange rate history since 1999</h2>
    <p>
      The chart shows the rate of the chosen pair on each ECB working day since 4 January 1999, the
      first day of the euro. Move the pointer over the chart, or focus it and use the arrow keys, to
      read the rate of a day. The figures under the chart give the change, the high and the low of
      the period.
    </p>
    <h2>Reference rates and the rate you pay</h2>
    <p>
      Reference rates are mid-market rates for information. Banks, card networks and exchange
      offices add a margin or a fee. To see the cost of a card payment, compare the amount on your
      statement with the result here.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
